(this is really long and contains a bunch of thoughts on Slash'EM and
a few on other nethack variants)

So this past week I've been sick. I was playing Crawl to kill time, 
dying every five minutes or so, but then I started doing well and
actually getting involved with it, and that was no good. So I figured
I would play some games of Slash'EM and splat them, and wandered over 
to slashem.crash-override.net to kill a few.

That didn't happen.

I had played S maybe a half-dozen times before --- all of them except
a samurai with an early wand of wishing had kicked it quickly, and the
Samurai ended up being killed by One-Eyed Sam. This game didn't come
with an early wand of wishing or indeed any early wishes, but it worked
out OK...

Valkyries in S, for those who don't know, start with a small dagger
stack, and a spear (at least I did). The spear is useless. The dagger
stack, though, is awesome. Even more awesome is that the sagger stack
can be combined with the "rustproof" and "enchant" features of a weapon
shop to get rustproof +1 daggers in the quiver right quick. That got me
through Sokoban (the new levels were fun, even if the lava was scary)
and to Minetown. Minetown was coaligned.

So, in normal, "vanilla" NetHack, Mjollnir adds d20 damage to 
non-shock-resistant creatures, as I recall. In S, it's a flat +24. WOW.
That's ludicrous. I wielded it for a bit, but was nervous about going
all the way down to Mines' End without MR, so sacrificed some more. 
Second gift? Magicbane. I used thrown daggers for damage and made it to
the luckstone, and also picke dup some valuable gems, which I identified
with shopkeeper services and sold back for protection money. It's like
the Sinister Device, except sanctioned!

(Apparently there was an additional level below Mines' End, but I didn't
run into the stairwell and never went back to check it out.)

After that I wandered down a bit more, noticed the Stronghold and decided
to ignore it, and found an altar on DL 14 and set up camp. I maxed my
luck and picked up the Gauntlets of Defense. Half physical damage this
early in the game? Wow! ID all of my scrolls, since I had picked up a 
magic marker --- what's this? Genocide? I reverse genocided silver dragons
to get myself SDSM, and it worked great. With Magicbane, I didn't have to 
worry about them hitting me at all. I also found I had charging, so at 
this point I had AC -20, MR, reflection, and genocide. If I had been
playing vanilla, I would have gone wishless and hurried up and ascended.

But instead, since I didn't know what was waiting for me, I remained
nervous. I did, though, realize that I could twoweapon, and this rendered
my dagger stack obsolete. Why? (insert statistics on +7 thrown daggers
blah blah blah) Because +0 Magicbane and +0 Mjollnir is ridiculously
overpowered. I probably averaged 45-50 damage per hit, and with max
luck, I never missed. Plus, I kept cancelling and probing and stunning
and confusing things! The damage might not have been as good, but +0
Magicbane was lots of fun, and I stuck with it until the end.

With this combination I went and did all of the Slash'EM branches.
(I also eventually did the role quest, which was uneventful, except for
having to be very careful to keep my cats off of the keyboard because I
didn't have levitation or water walking.)  My take on all of them:

 * Stronghold: This was a ton of fun because all the monsters were 
   awake when I got there and they had lots of interesting fortifications 
   to play with. I still got through it without too much trouble, but it
   was an enjoyable change from "a room full of monsters where all of them
   are asleep" or "a hallway." The soldiers didn't seem to use their new
   artillery very well --- they switched to knives at melee and preferred
   grenades to submachine guns when I could just walk away from the 
   grenades. Still, fun! 

 * Lawful Quest: Just silly, but fun silly. Doesn't belong in Vanilla but
   a fine slash'em addition.

 * Neutral Quest: Just easy. Also the Eye of the Beholder is useless.
 
 * Chaotic Quest: I stuck my nose in. Too many summoners!!! So I genocided
   most of them and came back. Vecna takes damage from +0 Magicbane, is that
   a feature?

 * Spiders: This was less fun. I stood on the stairs and hit buttons to 
   kill spiders until all the spiders were gone. The loot was nice, but 
   there wasn't much in the way of tactics to this one. Had I been less 
   powerful, I could have just spammed E until I reached a choke point.

 * Giants: This was more like the spiders than like the stronghold, though
   at least giants can use items.

 * Dragons: No scales! Lots of baby dragons! Lots of money! I didn't think
   this one was very challenging, though maybe I should have raised my XL
   before going in in order to get more real adult dragons. That could have
   been fun. :)

 * Sam: Cute, but once you know the wand of teleport trick, really pretty 
   easy. And oh my god the benefits that you get from this... I picked up 
   all the wands, rings, scrolls, and markers I could have wanted (except
   levitation, which I polied for). Maybe give him teleport control?
   This seemed too much like free everything to me. Of course, the only 
   other time I've made it past DL4 on S, I died to him, so...

 * Sunless Sea: I didn't bother getting the lamp. It's nice to have a 
   water level though. I also ran into my first displacer beast here ---
   the implementation is _really_ cute in an, I think, appropriately 
   NetHacky way.

I fell down past Medusa to the Castle, and I was actually really
disappointed that the Castle hadn't been changed around more. I think that
the drawbridge _needs_ to be nerfed, and the right way to do it is to
set up turret gunners in the castle towers. I think this should be done
even in Vanilla --- soldiers with wands would be appropriately powerful
there too, I think, or maybe give them bows. There'd have to be something
done to make it hard to return fire, and I've come up with an idea for that.

A new # type that works like iron bars but, the farther away from it
you are, the more shooting through it affects your to-hit roll. That way if
you were a precision archer you would be able to shoot through it from far
away, but if you were just chucking rocks, you'd be hopeless. Actually, do
iron bars already work this way? I've never experimented...

I would also toss them in the barracks facing the courtyard. As the game 
stands I don't ever have to wake those soldiers up unless I feel like it;
this time I didn't.

I went up to Medusa and the new Medusa level is really cool. There might
be more than one; this one had a bunch of small interconnected rooms with
iron bars for snakes to spit through. It also had asphynxes, which scared
me, but the one hissing I heard did nothing. I had to levelport up with
a random levelport to get back, because I still didn't have levitation.

At this point I had a 0:2 wand of wishing and the following holes:

 * no levitation
 * no MR not from wielded Magicbane

...
...

If I hadn't used an earlier Gypsy (dl35 or so) for a wish on an AoLS
(on the grounds that _something_ I hadn't expected would kill me), I
would have gone wishless. But instead I decided to try to wish for an
MR artifact and, if I didn't get it, wish for a CoMR so that I could
later use a cockatrice corpse if I cared to --- or, if I felt it necessary,
call up another AoLS. I didn't get the artifact, of course, and burned up
a conduct. Oh well.

I did the valley. Ho hum. Went downstairs and --- Demogorgon hits! You
feel sick! All _right_, this is why I started playing Slash'EM. Better
yet, he summoned incubi _and_ stunned me so that I couldn't write E. I
killed him and his summons (obviously, I guess, it is a YAAP) without
burning my amulet and then --- to my surprise --- discovered that no,
that's not supposed to happen. It was a bones level! ilya86's bones
level! His kit included the PYEC and Grayswandir, so until I woke the
wizard I twoweaponed GS and MJ. I was basically one-shotting all the &.
Even the named & didn't always get to teleport to the upstair. It
was completely ridonculous. I got through Gehennom quickly, skipping
Frankenstein because at this point I wanted to ascend and get the
oh-so-pretty

<Pallas> rax has played 1 games, between 20070211 and 20070215, 
         highest score 3881228, ascended 1 times

and I was getting bored. The endgame was uneventful. No force made it
really fast. Lichen on corpses meant that I didn't burn rations, which
was good, because I didn't have that many. The planes were the same,
and Astral was basically the same, except I felt like wands of teleport
didn't reach as far as I expected, but that might just have been me. More
powerful a in game meant I teleported them instead of smacked them but 
otherwise I didn't care.

All in all, I had fun. I liked that it was harder to get levels in the
early game. Later on, I felt like I could do too much damage, and that the
monsters weren't really very challenging, except maybe the crystal golem,
which, _damn_ that thing hit hard. I think --- and granted I've only tried
the game with a Valk, who shouldn't be too hard and I already know how
to play well --- that there's a little bit of a late-model Final Fantasy
problem where you do so much damage and have such good items that the
only way to make you nervous is monsters that are entirely overpowered.
I'm not sure what the way around that is.

I also think that the levels were best when monsters could hit me more
easily than I could hit them, rather than vice versa, and the monsters
were awake. In levels where the monsters generated asleep, I just killed
them one at a time. Easy as pie. I didn't take much advantage of new
item types, but most of them didn't seem that cool, except maybe potions
of invulnerability. (Amulets of stoning resistance also seemed cool but
not as cool as AoLS. Really, any amulet has to be pretty awesome for me
to wear it instead of typo resistance.) Artifact armor is really cool,
though I ascended with three sources of half physical damage and that
seems kind of silly.

Slash'EM does have some balance issues, but I think that some of the
ideas implemented in it are right on, and I think that for those of
us who feel like 3.4.3 is close to solved, it's worth some time. Being
able to play through dgamelaunch really reduces the effort threshhold
for me. I think it's a shame that the Grunthack server isn't running
anymore; if it were I would give it a shot. Perhaps I will anyway. I
think the potential for NH variants to be fun games is very high, and
also, given the things that have migrated from variants into vanilla in
the past few versions, obviously someone out there cares.

So --- anyone have any recommendations for good variants or patch 
collections? :)

-r.

